## The bar

What is being graded is whether the page reads as though this business paid a studio for it.
The thing to beat is the default: a centred hero over a gradient, three feature cards with
rounded corners and small icons, a pale grey section, a footer. That page is what a model
produces when asked for "a landing page", and it is recognisable at a glance.

What separates a real page from that one is mostly not decoration:

- **Structure carries the brand.** Vary section rhythm — full-bleed against inset, dense
  against open. A page where every section is a centred column of the same width reads as a
  template no matter how good the palette is.
- **Type does the work.** Commit to a real scale with genuine contrast between display and
  body. Set measure deliberately. Timid type is the single clearest tell.
- **Use the accent sparingly.** One accent, used a few times where it means something, reads
  as confidence. The same accent on every heading, button and border reads as a theme.
- **Motion follows the named motion language**, and only there. Nothing moves for the sake of
  moving, nothing loops forever, and everything respects
  `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)`.
- **Detail at the edges.** Considered focus states, sensible hover, real spacing rhythm.

Non-negotiable regardless of direction: body text meets WCAG AA against its background,
every interactive element is reachable and visible from the keyboard, inline SVG that carries
meaning has an accessible name, decorative SVG is hidden from assistive technology, the
document has one `<h1>` and a heading order that does not skip, and the page works from a
360px viewport upward.

The test to apply before you finish: imagine swapping in a generic logo from an unrelated
company. If the page still works, it is not this business's page yet — it is the default with
different words in it.
